  "topic": "How a load balancer keeps a website up when traffic spikes",
  "skill_id": "architecture_explainer",
  "learning_objective": "Explain how a load balancer spreads incoming requests across several servers and checks their health so a website stays up during traffic spikes.",
  "audience": "A beginner web user who knows websites run on servers but has never thought about what happens when many people visit at once.",
  "prerequisites": [],
  "brief": {
    "learner": "A beginner web user who knows websites run on servers but has never thought about what happens when many people visit at once.",
    "can_do": "Explain how a load balancer spreads incoming requests across several servers and checks their health so a website stays up during traffic spikes.",
    "question": "How does a website stay up when everyone visits at once?",
    "problem": "A ticket-selling site crashes the moment a popular concert goes on sale, even though it works fine on quiet days.",
    "naive_attempt": "A beginner thinks you just buy one bigger, faster server and everything will be fine, but a single server still has a fixed limit and is a single point of failure.",
    "turning_point": "A load balancer sits in front of many servers and shares each request, skipping any server that is down.",
    "check_question": "What does the load balancer do with a request when one server is down?",
    "check_answer": "It sends the request to another healthy server instead."
  },
